**Read:** [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), Blasius derivation and boundary-layer discussion; [Intermediate Fluid Mechanics — James Liburdy](../sources/Intermediate-Fluid-Mechanics-Liburdy.lingua#r_45KKWKB7DTN9MMQ951P62JHH5N), chapter VIII similarity solution; [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), high-speed boundary-layer notes.

For a steady laminar incompressible boundary layer on a stationary flat plate with uniform external speed U, choose η=y√[U/(νx)] and streamfunction ψ=√(νUx) f(η). Continuity is then satisfied, and the boundary-layer equation becomes f‴+(f f″)/2=0, with f(0)=f′(0)=0 and f′(∞)=1. Other normalizations move factors of two; compare the definition of η before comparing constants.

The numerical value f″(0) is approximately 0.332057336. Thus the local skin-friction coefficient, referenced to ρU²/2, is approximately 0.664114672/√Re_x. Integrating the similarity profile gives the displacement and momentum thicknesses and an independent momentum-integral relation. These quantities offer stronger checks than one plotted profile.

Derive the reduction, solve the shooting or boundary-value problem, and vary both numerical spacing and the finite approximation to infinity. Recover the near-wall series and the far-field slope. The result assumes the stated laminar, zero-pressure-gradient, thin-layer regime; neither the leading-edge singular idealization nor a downstream transition is resolved by the similarity solution.

